Here's how to implement this in .NET MAUI Shell:

## 1. Override `OnBackButtonPressed` on your page

```csharp
public partial class EditProductPage : ContentPage
{
    private bool _hasUnsavedChanges;

    protected override bool OnBackButtonPressed()
    {
        if (_hasUnsavedChanges)
        {
            // Fire-and-forget the async confirmation
            Dispatcher.Dispatch(async () =>
            {
                bool leave = await DisplayAlert(
                    "Unsaved Changes",
                    "You have unsaved changes. Discard them?",
                    "Discard", "Stay");

                if (leave)
                    await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
            });

            return true; // Cancel the default back navigation
        }

        return false; // Allow normal navigation
    }
}
```

Returning `true` suppresses the hardware/software back button. The dispatcher lets you show the async alert from a synchronous override.

## 2. Custom "Cancel" back button in the toolbar

In your XAML:

```xml
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             x:Class="MyApp.EditProductPage"
             Shell.BackButtonBehavior="{x:Null}">

    <Shell.BackButtonBehavior>
        <BackButtonBehavior Command="{Binding CancelCommand}"
                           TextOverride="Cancel"
                           IconOverride="" />
    </Shell.BackButtonBehavior>

    <!-- page content -->
</ContentPage>
```

## 3. ViewModel command with confirmation

```csharp
[RelayCommand]
private async Task Cancel()
{
    if (_hasUnsavedChanges)
    {
        bool leave = await Shell.Current.DisplayAlert(
            "Unsaved Changes",
            "You have unsaved changes. Discard them?",
            "Discard", "Stay");

        if (!leave) return;
    }

    await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
}
```

## Key points

| Concern | Mechanism |
|---------|-----------|
| Hardware back (Android) | `OnBackButtonPressed()` returning `true` |
| Shell back button appearance | `BackButtonBehavior.TextOverride` / `IconOverride` |
| Custom tap logic | `BackButtonBehavior.Command` |
| iOS swipe-back gesture | Set `Shell.BackButtonBehavior` with `IsEnabled="True"` — the command intercepts it |

> **Note:** On iOS, the interactive swipe-back gesture bypasses `OnBackButtonPressed`. Using `BackButtonBehavior.Command` handles both the button tap and the gesture on iOS 16+. For older iOS versions you may need a custom renderer to disable the gesture entirely.
